@barbeau Re: start_date + GTFS.

Fun fact: if your transit system has a bus starting at 00:30:00 local wall time, then the only proper way I know to encode such bus in GTFS (static) is by defining it as 24:30:00 of the previous day. It is otherwise impossible to encode this bus on a day when time is moved back by 1 hour (that is, the calendar day, when 13 hours pass between 00:00 wall time and 12:00 wall time) -- GTFS definition of time is 12 hours before local noon, which corresponds to 1am wall time for that day, and you'd need negative time to encode such a trip at such a day; GTFS 00:30:00 means wall time 01:30:00 on such day (assuming a typical switch-over time between 2am and 4am). In the same way on the short day (moving time forward), GTFS 00:30:00 will correspond to 23:30:00 wall time of the previous day. If I were to give a recommendation, I'd recommend to always provide times in GTFS (static) feeds in the time window of 03:00:00-27:00:00 (or 02:00:00-26:00:00, depending on when the typical DST-switchover happens in the system), because these times correspond to wall times on every day of the year.

Now, back to GTFS RT. Resolving start_time's of 24:00:00+ without start_date is not very fruitful (as in: possible, but may trigger ambiguity). Therefore I'd also encourage start_date to be specified properly in order to avoid potential ambiguity.

I think we should bring the other existing producers into this discussion, as their willingness to adopt to a new format for something they have been previously publishing is important.

Does anyone have good contacts at the existing producers?  I don't have direct contacts at LA Metro, Madrid, and NY/New Jersey PATH, but I believe some of my colleagues do.

@antrim How many agencies are you currently publishing station entrances for in the existing format?

Spec:
> "You must specify arrival and departure times for the first and last stops in a trip."

@barbeau @devinbraun : This has bothered me for years, because it means that we sometimes have to supply meaningless values for first arrival or last departure time. Should we open up an issue to discuss this? I think we'd need to find out what applications require this and work with them if we were to propose a change which is not backwards-compatible.

@antrim Thanks!  I support this proposal, although I think something went wrong with the formatting in these changes - check out https://github.com/trilliumtransit/transit/blob/9683278f015ecb32a78f8a493fcc9ab43f0ea105/gtfs/spec/en/reference.md.  It looks like all fields after `arrival_time` aren't in the table format.

Also, it seems like there is a duplicate section of "If this time isn't a timepoint" under `arrival_time`.  I'd recommend cutting the middle paragraph in the changes and making sure all those points are in the following paragraph?

Also, there is a sentence `Provide arrival times for all stops that are time points.`  I think this should be changed to `Provide arrival and departure times for all stops that are time points.`?

[tl;dr at the bottom, sorry]

I haven't thought it through, but at first glance, I'm very reluctant in adding any onboarding information in the `shapes.txt`. I understand that — even if we put it in `stop_times.txt` — the shape will play an important role. But putting `pickup` and `drop_off` values in there makes me uncomfortable.

As a GTFS consumer, we often have to drop the original shapes by creating new cleaned ones. Adding extra information on some shape points of the shape breaks the whole idea of a shape just being... well... a shape.

Also, what will be the name of that area? We won't be able to add a `stop_name` anymore, so how will it be displayed in the stop list? If we add then in `shapes.txt`, I'm afraid we will have to add odd things like `section_name` or `area_name` to be able to put a name to that continuous stop, same thing for `stop_url`, `stop_desc`...

@mgilligan you say "It forces a producer to supply shapes.txt to add continuous stops.": I rather have a producer not providing a shapes.txt, and implying that all the continuous stops are a straight line between the start and the end, rather than having a producer supplying an awful `shapes.txt`, which doesn't make sense. For example, a file just providing the portion of the shape which are continuous stops.

@mgilligan you say "In all cases I've seen, 'continuous' applies to all trips following the pattern so it could live in shapes.txt [...]". I am already expecting that some agency will add some continuous stop for one trip, and therefore add it for the shape, and down the road won't realize that they have added it to all the trips describe by that shape.

The title of that discussion is "Add continuous/request stops". And I think we are indeed still speaking about stops of some kind. Therefore we should still rely on `stops.txt` and all the fields that we have created in the file...

[tl;dr:] I would rather see that in `stop_times.txt`, because IMHO:
(i) it reduces the risk of agency breaking everything (1 stop_time - 1 trip vs 1 shape - n trips);
(ii) it allows the GTFS consumers to keep on relying on existing shape-improving tools;
(iii) it allows to reuse the work done on defining the concept of stops (name, code, desc, fares, stop_timezone, parent station, ...);
(iv) is more consistent with the spirit of the GTFS (that last one may just be in my mind, I agree).

@barbeau thanks for the clarifications, and of course I am happy to see GTFS being used in new ways. I'd say the key question/issue is not the relevance to the "typical" consumer (who we don't know) but just clarity on what a timepoint is and means. "Strictly adhere" is poorly defined, yet the same expression is applied to both arrivals and departures. This does not give much information to the GTFS consumer about how to interpret and make use of a timepoint field.

I think it is fairly common that the operator is in fact not bound to adhere to the arrival time at a timepoint, and "late" arrival is not sanctioned. Consider the case you mentioned, where there's a 10 minute layover and the vehicle arrives 8 minutes late. It's entirely possible that this is considered normal (not late) operation, as the operator is given 10 minutes of slack at this timepoint to compensate for traffic conditions. @skinkie isn't this how buses function in the Netherlands?

So again, we do need language explaining that buses will hold for departure, but I'm not sure there's agreement across all networks about how vehicles are constrained by timepoint arrival times.

@antrim the original language makes sense to me, I think it just needs more clarification. As @sabre23t points out, when roads are not curved geometry points may be only at intersections, or even farther apart. @skinkie's interpretation is the same I've seen in most GTFS consumers, that the stops are projected onto the shape geometry using the shortest possible line. 

In most cases stop locations cannot touch the shape exactly, because the shape is re-used for both directions of the route, following the centerline of the road with the stops more accurately located off to the sides. As @antrim points out, forcing the shape to touch all the stops would make the shape zig-zag across the road, sometimes even having sawtooth shapes where stops are across from one another. This is clearly bad, so some form of this statement needs to remain.

The confusion here is between the points and the geometry. I would say: "The points do not need to match stop locations, but all stops on a trip should lie within a small distance of straight line segments between the shape points for that trip."

@skinkie Is your point that it is not needed in the Netherlands, or that it is needed but the value currently defined do not describe properly the reality?

The way I would understand the current spec with `stop_name` beeing just the name of the perpendicular street, is that either it is NS, so the stop is just before the street, or FS, so the stop is just after the street.

But we can definitely add new values if you think there is countries where we would need them.

I had a similar initial reaction to @skinkie, that this may too closely reflect a practice that is very specific to certain agencies or towns. Even riding transit in the US I don't recall ever seeing a stop designated this way (near-side, far-side). However, it does seem like a useful piece of information that is potentially applicable anywhere landmarks and street intersections are used as stop names, and could be helpful to riders even in places where this is not a common naming scheme. 

@skinkie, naming a bus stop "Street X" with the additional field relative_position=FS means the bus stop is after the perpendicular Street X in the direction of travel; relative_position=NS means that the bus stops before the perpendicular Street X in the direction of travel. This seems meaningful any time a cross street is used as a station name.

The only other place I've seen this is in Singapore where it's very common to name bus stops "OPP X Park" or "OPP BLK 298B" to mean "across the street from" the named landmark, but the OPP is part of the stop name that everyone would know.

@abyrd I have not included the `cardinal_direction` field in my pull request. I have only included the `relative_position` one. Therefore I won't start the conversation about the `cardinal_direction` field in this thread.

@skinkie The four options are the ones which always appear in all the cases I saw. I didn't know that Madison was doing it, and the values that @tesobota gave us are those exact 4 ones.

The pro for an ENUM instead of a STRING are:
- field translation and localization;
- no need for a "readable" (or tts) version of the field;
- consistency between the producers ("adjacent" vs "at position" or "NS" vs "Near Side");
- the choice between showing abbreviation or desabbreviation is up to the GTFS-consumer.

=> Therefore I would tend to keep it as an ENUM.

But I may be missing cases that it won't handle. What do you have in mind when you say that a localization description would be better?

I agree with @LeoFR that the GTFS zip should contain the PLS file.  Otherwise consumers are going to need to monitor more than one link to determine if something changed and they need to update their data.

I think we should start looking for producers for this proposal.  If we can find one that's interested in putting together a PLS document (and a consumer that's able to consume it) then we can include that in the spec.  If not, I propose that we just adopt the plain text field for now (assuming we get the producers and consumers), and we can revisit the PLS document when the need arises for a producer.

@LeoFR Did you have any producers in mind?  I can reach out to a few as well.

@DaveBarker Would you be interested in producing the plain text?  How about the PLS?

Yeah, I'm thinking that directly providing SSML probably makes more sense, as consumers would need to convert to that anyway.

@LeoFR IDs in the GTFS with a mapping to elements in a separate SSML file that contains the actual SSML would work.  The only other option I can think of off the top of my head would be to embed the `<phoneme alphabet="ipa" ph="ˈdʊstər">Dorcester</phoneme> Station` directly into a `stop_name_ssml` field in the GTFS file, although that comes with all the caveats we've previously mentioned about XML in a CSV.  (An aside for this - I'm thinking we probably only need the text that's within the `<speak>` tags (not the entire XML file) - it looks like that is what both [Alexa](https://developer.amazon.com/public/solutions/alexa/alexa-skills-kit/docs/speech-synthesis-markup-language-ssml-reference) and [Google Assistant](https://developers.google.com/actions/reference/ssml) need as input.  But, we still have the XML/CSV issues).

@DaveBarker Would @LeoFR's proposal above with IDs in GTFS field mapping to separate SSML file work for you?

@barbeau If I may make a tiny tiny remark, the specification currently contains almost only single-space after a full stop (37 single space vs 1 double-space for `gtfs/CHANGES.md`).

Your PR contains a double-space after a full stop.

😄

@skinkie Producers should always include data in the feed if they have it - I definitely won't argue with that.  But I don't think it makes sense to have producers fill in data for values they don't know.  There is definitely room for clarifying how consumers should present such unknown information.  For example, in OneBusAway is a real-time arrival/departure prediction exists, we show that information to the user, and if it does not, then we show the scheduled time to the user (alternately, an app could just show "UNKNOWN").  I'd certainly be supportive of clarifying expected behavior for consumers to create less fragmented end user experience.

Back to the above case, though - in the scenario you present, how would you suggest a producer populate the `arrival` prediction field in GTFS-rt that is unknown?  IMHO it's better to leave the field empty than create a value that isn't a legitimate value a transit rider should see.  

For example, if we require providers fill both fields, I think most providers would (or do) copy arrival to departure, and vice versa.  But this creates false expectations for transit riders by telling them a vehicle will arrive or depart when it may not actually do that.

Hi @RachM, it does seem like a good idea to describe the composition of multi-carriage trains. Many rail travelers would appreciate this kind of information. 

In an existing system in the Netherlands two additional fields are present: train-equipment-type and train-equipment-code. In many cases these imply the other amenity fields so are somewhat redundant, but it's could imply additional detail (I'm thinking of double-deck trains or large seatless areas near doors). In a particularly good implementation, it may actually allow you to visually recognize the train in the station. Check out https://drgl.nl/stop/NL:S:ut and click on any train to see its status with images and names for the carriage types, derived from such fields.

@skinkie or @skywave might be able to give you more information about this data source.

@barbeau: I agree that we have to specify the ordering. I added that portion to my proposal. My bad.

About the right-to-left vs left-to-right, I would tend to think it is overkill to speak about it in the spec. We are defining that such route should be "before" such other route, which is enough IMHO at the spec level.

But maybe it should be in the Best Practices?

+1  Don't see a problem with it. And in my own implementation cater for it

              // If only one of arrival or departure is specified in StopTimeUpdate, let the other assume its value
              arrival = departure = null;
              if (stoptimeUpdate.hasArrival()) arrival = stoptimeUpdate.getArrival();
              if (stoptimeUpdate.hasDeparture()) departure = stoptimeUpdate.getDeparture();
              if (arrival == null  || (!arrival.hasTime() && !arrival.hasDelay())) arrival = departure;
              if (departure == null  || (!departure.hasTime() && !departure.hasDelay())) departure = arrival;

@skinkie with your comment in mind I've re-read this whole comment chain. To summarize: 

@barbeau said of a feed missing arrival predictions: "we either need to flag this feed as having errors, or remove the text requiring producers to always populate both arrival and departure (i.e., this proposal)." This is the core issue: is this feed wrong, or is the spec wrong?

In his comment https://github.com/google/transit/pull/65#issuecomment-337032447 he points out that requiring both arrival and departure times contradicts existing text in the specification. Since specification coherency is obviously a requirement, rejecting the present proposal implies another change where the rest of the spec is changed to always require both arrivals and departures.

So @skinkie your rejection of this proposal seems to be to be a small element of a larger, more general objective that has perhaps not been completely articulated: eliminate missing information from all RT feeds. Your response to the question above would be that the feed is wrong, should be considered invalid, and all GTFS-RT producers should be updated to follow the revised spec.

The logic behind that broad change is that all stop time updates are predictions rather than observations, and are coming out of a system that was designed to perform predictions. Such systems should fully assume their role and provide complete predictions using principled methods, rather than forcing countless consumers to perform their own ad-hoc / amateur propagation.

Broadly I'm in agreement with this architectural position -- it's one that we debated in the Netherlands where we all came to the conclusion that @skinkie is advocating. But I don't know how realistic it is to require such modifications to RT producing systems at this stage.

