@gcamp I wouldn't call this as much of an interpretation of the spec but more design driven by end-user requirements in OneBusAway.  If a producer only provides real-time info on the current trip, it's a really bad user experience if that information isn't propagated to subsequent trips in the same block.  As mentioned above, users see real-time "pop in" right before the bus reaches their stop, while the system actually contained that information much further in advance.

I agree that in an ideal world producers would provide high-quality predictions for all trips and stops the vehicle is currently assigned to, but from what I've seen we have a way to go before we get there.  In the meantime this PR helps define what the expected behavior should be for these systems.  

Looking forward, though, perhaps in the spec or in GTFS-realtime Best Practices we should advocate more strongly for including predictions across multiple stops and trips when high-quality information is available?

@barbeau you would want to show departure that are marked as `REALTIME_IMPRECISE`, even if they aren't as precise as other departures. It's imprecise, but still better than schedule from the GTFS. `show_to_user` or similar doesn't achieve the same goal here. 

The reason I would push for `UPDATED_SCHEDULE` is that updating schedule information is often done by the same system that pushes real time trip updates. It feels a bit wasteful not to use the spec we already have to update the schedule, since it's very very close to what it already achieves. 

That said, I'm not against having a single flag that denote if the departure is precise enough to be shown a real time symbol if that would fit everyone and move quicker.

@archerian To my knowledge there isn't a way to both indicate that a vehicle is before stop B but will skip stop B and go straight to stop C in a VehiclePosition feed.  You could just have `VehicleStopStatus` with `IN_TRANSIT_TO` station C, but then you're missing the information that the vehicle is actually currently between A and B.  

Do you have a strong requirement to include this info directly in the VehiclePositions feed?

As of today, in GTFS-realtime you can use a TripUpdate stop_time_update with `schedule_relationship: SKIPPED`, or an Alert with an Effect of `NO_SERVICE` for that stop_id and trip_id.

For example, the TripUpdate:

~~~
trip_update {
    trip {
      trip_id: "1234"
    }
    stop_time_update {
      stop_sequence: 1
      arrival {
        time: 1539037800
      }
      departure {
        time: 1539037800
      }
      stop_id: "A"
    }
    stop_time_update {
      stop_sequence: 2
      arrival {
        time: 1539037906
      }
      departure {
        time: 1539037906
      }
      stop_id: "B"
      schedule_relationship: SKIPPED
    }
    stop_time_update {
      stop_sequence: 3
      arrival {
        time: 1539038040
      }
      departure {
        time: 1539038040
      }
      stop_id: "C"
    }
~~~

Alert would look like:

~~~
    informed_entity {
      stop_id: "B"
      trip_id: "1234"
    }
    effect: NO_SERVICE
~~~

Note that we've also drafted a GTFS-ServiceChanges spec which would handle this case by modifying the trip itself:
http://bit.ly/gtfs-service-changes

This is currently in draft format but if you think it would meet your needs please let us know.

