Just to back up a step, I'd like to give data examples of the migration that we're talking about for the use case of the "copy/paste" trip we're trying to address with `DUPLICATED` - taking an existing static GTFS trip and running a duplicate of it at a new date/time. 

*Note that `ADDED` trips are currently not well-defined in GTFS-RT and are used many different ways - we're talking about standardizing just one use case of `ADDED` here.*

Here's an example of how duplicated trips are currently being hacked into `ADDED` trip_updates:

~~~
entity {
  id: "ei0"
  trip_update {
    trip: {
      trip_id: "TripId0" // <-- trip_id from static GTFS to copy
      schedule_relationship: ADDED
      start_date: "20190821" // <-- This would normally point to static, but instead defines the new trip date
      start_time: "11:30:00" // <-- This would normally point to static, but instead defines the new trip time
    }
    stop_time_update {
	...
    }
  }
}
~~~

...and here's how it would look with the new `DUPLICATED` proposal:

~~~
entity {
  id: "ei10"
  trip_update {
    trip: {
      trip_id: "TripId0" // <-- trip_id from static GTFS to copy
      schedule_relationship: DUPLICATED
    }
    trip_properties {
      trip_id: "AddedTripId1" // new trip_id unique to this trip
      start_date: "20190821" // new trip date
      start_time: "11:30:00"   // new trip time
    }
    stop_time_update {
	...
    }
  }
}
~~~

As I mentioned above, one relatively simple migration strategy here is to just have both messages side-by-side in the feed. As long as `trip.trip_id` points to the same static `trip_id` in both cases, legacy consumers will ignore `DUPLICATED` trips and new consumers can ignore `ADDED` trips that have the same `trip_id` as `DUPLICATED` trips. This avoids producers from having to set up an entirely new production GTFS-RT endpoint for switching over. 

I would suggest explicitly adding this migration guide to the spec, like:

>Migration Guide: For producers that have implemented duplicated trips via ADDED, when DUPLICATED trips are added to the feed the corresponding ADDED trip should have the same `trip.trip_id`. Consumers will then ignore any ADDED trips that have the same `trip.trip_id` as a DUPLICATED trip.

@paulswartz Thoughts on this as a producer?

This isn't how we use `ADDED` trips, even in the situation where we're actually duplicating a trip with a different start time.

For some trips, it's not based on anything from static GTFS (other than stops + routes, I suppose). These get an ID based on a timestamp so that they're unique, but otherwise don't refer to a trip from GTFS.

For overloaded trips, where we're creating a new trip by adjusting the start time (and also running the original trip), it looks like this:
```
entity {
  id: "overload1"
  trip_update {
    trip: {
      trip_id: "TripId0-Overload1" 
      schedule_relationship: ADDED
      start_date: "20190821" 
      start_time: "11:30:00"
    }
    stop_time_update {
	...
    }
  }
}
```

The issue with the `DUPLICATED` trip is that now the `trip_id` is the static trip ID, not the new trip ID, and I worry about consumers being confused:
```
