@muto-jo Good question! If you're asking the question "what fields do I need to provide for my feed to be valid GTFS-realtime?", then you need to follow the requirements in the reference document. These are the semantic requirements of the spec. 

The definitions in the GTFS-realtime .proto field are the requirements of the protocol buffer encoding, which, based on [quirks of protocol buffers](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/proto#specifying_field_rules), means that all future fields will be marked as "optional" even if they are defined as required semantically (in the reference document). So there are and will be differences between these two documents.

See the top of the ["Required" section](https://github.com/google/transit/blob/master/gtfs-realtime/spec/en/reference.md#required) in the reference documentation for details, including a link to the proposal that defined the semantic requirements for GTFS-realtime.

@flocsy In response to the pandemic, we've implemented in our ITS a ratio system that allows to differenciate between maximal occupancy per vehicle type from desired occupancy. 

Say a given bus model has 45 seats, but to respect the two meter rule, you want to limit occupancy to 20 seats, our users can set a ratio (in %) of maximal occupancy that will drive the GTFS-RT occupancy status, coupled with our people counter data. 

In this case, the 45 seat bus would report "standing room only" if 20 people were on the bus at that time.

