Regarding the compatibility: right now Swiftly allows consumers to request the GTFS-rt trip updates feed with or without trip-modifications. If a consumer requests the feed without trip-modifications then we keep the normal trip ids and simply mark stops skipped by a detour as "skipped". 

If a consumer can support trip-modifications then they fetch the version that has this feature enabled and will get more details on the detour shape, temp stops, etc. We're definitely open to ideas if there's a way to better support backward compatibility _with_ the trip-modifications feature enabled. The approach we have now seems to be working well for us so far.

After internal discussions and with @abyrd, here are the three options that we think are worth proposing to the community. 

We will add a new StopSelector object that would select a stop like TripUpdates does now : 

```
message StopSelector { 
  // Select a stop by stop sequence or by stop_id. At least one of the two values must be provided.
  optional uint32 stop_sequence = 1;
  optional string stop_id = 2;
}
```

To select the actual range of stops that is modified by the TripModification we have three options. All of those would be inside the `Modification` object. The first 3 options assume that stops cannot be cancelled then reintroduced by ReplacementStop, in other words it enforce that the stop listed must be cancelled. 

#### ❤️ StopSelector with number of stop

```
  // The first stop that is to be affected by this modification.
  optional StopSelector start_stop = 1; 
  // The number of stops which are canceled and replaced by the replacement_stops. 
  //May be zero to indicate no skipped stops.
  optional uint32 num_stops_replaced = 2; 
```

####  🚀 StopSelector for first and last selected stop

```
  // The first stop that is to be affected by this modification.
  optional StopSelector start_stop = 1;
  // The last stop that is to be affected by this modification
  // All stops inclusively between `start_stop` and `end_stop` are canceled and replaced by the replacement_stops. 
  // If no `end_stop` is provided, there will be no skipped stops.  
  optional StopSelector end_stop = 2; 
```

#### 👀   List of StopSelector

```
  // The list of stops that are cancelled by this modification
  repeated StopSelector target_stops = 1;
```
This last option wouldn't allow modification with no stops canceled. 

#### 🎉  Add the ability to select stops by the selector that are not cancelled

Select this option if you want the ability to replace any stops, even those that are not cancelled like Andrew described [here](https://github.com/google/transit/pull/403#issuecomment-1913130432). Can be combined with any of the first three options. 

### Next steps 

I would like to invite the community to vote on what they think is the best option by **reacting with the corresponding emoji to this message**. If you vote "🎉  Add the ability to cancel stops", feel free to also add an other vote for the method of selection of stop range. If you have more a nuanced comment, don't hesitate to respond with a regular message.

I think this was the last outstanding item to get past. If you have any further comments, please do so soon! **We plan to implement the changes done since September and call a vote soon after.**

